  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nIR shows cooling cloud tops across the CWA as the tropical\nmoisture has begun to interact with the open wave pushing up\nfrom the southwest nearing the CWA. WV imagery shows a\ndisjointed PFJ dipping around the trough to the west but mainly\nto the north into southern Canada with a 120-130kt jet max\nhelping to aid the aforementioned system that will affect the \nCWA throughout Friday and lingering into Saturday. Radar shows a\nfew scattered light showers pushing across Natrona County that \nwill diminish before sunrise Friday morning. The main push of \nprecipitation will come from eastern Idaho and northern Utah \nlater through the overnight hours that will eventually spread \neast to the Divide later in the morning. Some rumbles of thunder\nare possible with instability values in the 100-200 J/kg range \ninto the afternoon. PVA anomalies look to be more constrained \nover Idaho and into southern Montana, limiting any significant \naccumulations but limited mountain snow is likely for the higher\nelevations of the western mountains. More prominent for the \nTetons and into Yellowstone, 3 to 5 inches are possible but with\nlower confidence at this time (30-40%). Freezing levels will \nremain above 10kft ahead of the cold front later in the \nafternoon with limited precipitation at that time of FROPA. \n\nWinds will be of concern today remaining elevated in many areas\ninto Friday morning already, with a possible weak mountain wave\noff the southern Winds into the eastern parts of the Wind River\nBasin. Gusts up to 30 to 35 mph are possible with this but not\nof high concern at this time. Winds will increase through the\nlate Friday morning hours as the gradient continues to tighten\nahead of FROPA. Southwest gusts up to 40 to 55 mph are likely\nfor much of the area, strongest east of the Divide. HiRes models\nindicate the front to push into Yellowstone by around 4PM and \npushing southeast through the evening hours, reaching east of \nthe Bighorns by 6-7PM where the highest of fire weather concerns\nwill be. Moisture will be limited east of the Divide, but areas\naround the Cody Foothills off the Absarokas will have the best \nchance for precipitation with frontal passage, outside of the \nupslope flow of the Bighorns to Owl Creeks/Bridgers. Minimal \nhigh elevation snow and limited in the valley rain of \nsignificance but around a 20 percent coverage on this occurrence \nheading towards sunset Friday evening. By sunrise Saturday \nmorning, any shower activity will have diminish in wake of the \ncold front as this system has continued to under perform with \nthe models with its weaker trajectory over time this past week. \nBeing indicative of the winter to come in the stronger El Nino \npattern, it could be a recurring theme going forward and \nespecially east of the Divide. \n\nBeyond that, temperatures Friday afternoon will push 10 to 20\ndegree above average due to the suppresional heating ahead of\nthe aforementioned cold front. Behind it, expect more seasonable\nto just below average temperatures over the weekend into early\nnext week to feel more Fall-like heading into the last week of\nOctober in Wyoming. Behind this quick pushing system into the\nnorthern Great Plains, expect induce ridging for the later half\nof the weekend ahead of the next bowling ball of a low coming\nfrom the Gulf of Alaska. By Monday morning, expect it to reach\nthe Pacific northwest remaining a closed low that will begin to\naffect the CWA and in particular for the west by Tuesday. This\nwill be more inclined for western mountain snow and reinforcing\nthe cooler air to allow for even some valley snow west of the\nDivide. Time will tell, but models show a disjointed system with\nmuch disagreement still to come. Stay tuned for updated\nforecasts beyond this system for Friday in the coming days.",
